# DDIA labs

A lab for reproducing DDIA anomalies, not a production system.
The bulletin board (`app/`) is the real application. Simulation-only code lives under
`fake/`; `fake/database` models the cluster and `fake/simulator` executes experiments.
Labs own only scenarios and invariant checks.

## Simulation layers

- Dependencies are acyclic: `fake/database` uses domain types from `app`,
  `fake/transport` uses `fake/database`, and `fake/simulator` composes them.
- Routing lives in `fake/database/`, not in `app`. The real board does not name fake replicas.
- `fake/database.Cluster` applies operations only to an explicit `Location`; it never selects one.
- `fake/transport.Transport` sends to one explicit destination; it never chooses replication targets.
- If reproducing an infrastructure anomaly requires changing the domain, the layer split is wrong.
- Partitioning is an infrastructure policy, not a domain concept. The board is one board.

## Simulation experiments

- Anomalies must emerge. Do not hand-place lag; only `fake/simulator.Sim.Advance` moves replication forward.
- Countermeasures live in `fake/database/router.go` and the fake session. They must be swappable so the same
  invariant flips between fail and pass.
- One RNG stream per layer (workload / delay / routing / fault). Never share one stream —
  swapping a router must not perturb delays or workload.
- Checkers read `fake/simulator.Event` history only. Never widen the real app to expose fake ground truth.
- Add a mechanism only when it lets a named invariant fail. Not because it exists in reality.

## Phases

- Phase 1: deterministic single-threaded simulation. Virtual clock, fixed seed.
- Phase 2: real concurrency outside the fake packages. Keep timestamped history and fault-injection
  seeds so failures stay analyzable. Move when the checker is calibrated, not on a schedule.

## Real PostgreSQL SQL lab

- `infra/postgres/` and `labs/postgres_replication/` are a separate SQL-only lab.
  Start with its README and `make db-up` / `make db-test`; do not require a Go build.
- PostgreSQL owns physical replication; no simulated transport, virtual clock, or
  partitioning. Intentional WAL replay pause/resume is the fault injection here.
- Confirm actual `paused` state, compare replay LSN with the post-commit token,
  and verify row visibility. Use bounded polling; SQL errors fail the test.
- Always attempt to resume test-owned pauses on failure/interruption. Do not
  undo pauses already present before a test. No concurrent replay-control tests.
- Scope Docker commands to `ddia-replication-lag`, bind ports only to loopback,
  and preserve data on normal shutdown. Credentials are public local-lab-only.
- Never silently erase/reinitialize partial data or failed replication slots.
  Document explicit reset of the three dedicated volumes; never use broad prune.
- Real read routing is separate from `fake/database/router.go`.

## Style

- Name tests after the anomaly or invariant they show (`ReadAfterWrite`, `MonotonicReads`).
- Do not chase production quality: no connection pooling, metrics, or "realistic" networking
  for its own sake.
- Do not summarize book chapters in code comments. State only the experiment's contract.
